Choosing between COPY INTO and Snowpipe depends on your data arrival frequency, latency requirements, and how you want to manage compute resources.
------------------------------
## COPY INTO vs. Snowpipe: Core Differences
The fundamental difference is that COPY INTO <table_name> is an explicitly triggered batch command, while Snowpipe is an automated, serverless service designed to run that command in the background as soon as files appear. [1, 2] 

| Feature | COPY INTO | Snowpipe |
|---|---|---|
| Ingestion Type | Batch (Scheduled or Manual) | Continuous (Event-Driven) |
| Compute Type | User-managed Virtual Warehouse | Serverless (Snowflake-managed compute) |
| Billing Model | Billed per warehouse second | Billed per file based on data volume & processing |
| Target Latency | Minutes to Hours (Depends on schedule) | Near Real-Time (Seconds to Minutes) |
| Optimal File Size | Large batches (100 MB–250 MB+ compressed) | Small micro-batches (100 MB–250 MB compressed) |
| Transaction Boundary | File-level (Fails/rolls back per file by default) | Micro-batch chunk level (May split file commits) |

## When to use COPY INTO:

* 
* You ingest data on a fixed schedule (e.g., nightly, hourly) using an orchestrator like Airflow or [Snowflake Tasks](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/tasks-intro).
* You are processing massive batch files and want to control costs by sizing your own warehouse (e.g., using a Large or X-Large warehouse to maximize parallelism).
* You require strict file-level transaction boundaries where partial loads of a specific file are unacceptable. [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7] 
* 

## When to use Snowpipe:

* 
* Data files arrive sporadically or continuously in cloud storage throughout the day.
* You want low latency (data available within seconds/minutes of cloud storage arrival).
* You want a hands-off approach without managing or resizing virtual warehouses. [2, 5, 7, 8] 
* 

