## How Continuous Ingestion Works
Continuous ingestion in Snowflake automatically moves data from an external stage (Amazon S3, Google Cloud Storage, or Azure Blob Storage) into target tables without manual intervention. [1, 8] 

[ Data Source ] ──> [ Cloud Storage Bucket ] ──( Event Notification )──> [ Snowpipe ] ──> [ Snowflake Table ]


   1. File Landing: An application or streaming process drops a file into a cloud storage landing zone. [8] 
   2. Event Trigger: The cloud provider triggers a storage event notification (e.g., AWS SQS/SNS, Google Pub/Sub, Azure Event Grid). Alternatively, you can trigger Snowpipe manually via its REST API endpoint. [5, 9] 
   3. Queueing: Snowpipe detects the event and places the file into an ingestion queue. [1] 
   4. Serverless Execution: Snowflake allocates internal, serverless compute resources to run the underlying COPY INTO statement against the queued files. [5, 7] 
   5. Idempotency (Load History): Snowpipe checks the metadata history of the pipe. If a file name matches one processed in the last 64 days, it is skipped to prevent duplicate records. [6] 

