## How to Build Near Real-Time Data Pipelines
To build an efficient end-to-end, near real-time ingestion pipeline in Snowflake, combine Snowpipe Auto-Ingest with Streams and Tasks or Dynamic Tables for continuous data transformation.
## Step 1: Create an External Stage
Establish a connection to your cloud storage bucket.

CREATE OR REPLACE STAGE my_s3_stage
  URL = 's3://my-bucket/incoming/'
  STORAGE_INTEGRATION = my_s3_integration;

## Step 2: Define the Snowpipe
Create a wrapper object that defines the exact COPY INTO mechanism. Ensure AUTO_INGEST = TRUE is flagged. [5, 9] 

CREATE OR REPLACE PIPE raw_data_pipe
  AUTO_INGEST = TRUE
  AS
  COPY INTO landing_raw_table
  FROM @my_s3_stage
  FILE_FORMAT = (TYPE = 'JSON');

## Step 3: Configure Cloud Event Notifications
Run SHOW PIPES; and copy the ARN/URL from the notification_channel column. Paste this into your cloud provider's event settings (e.g., S3 Event Notifications) so that any ObjectCreated event sends a message directly to that queue. [5, 9] 
## Step 4: Implement Continuous CDC (Change Data Capture)
Once data lands in your landing_raw_table, you need to transform it. You have two modern architectural choices for near real-time transformation:

* 
* Option A: Streams & Tasks (Imperative): Create a [Snowflake Stream](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/streams-intro) on your raw table to capture append-only logs. Run a Snowflake Task every minute that executes an INSERT/MERGE statement only if the stream contains new data. [3] 
* Option B: Dynamic Tables (Declarative): Create a [Snowflake Dynamic Table](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/dynamic-tables-about) pointing to your raw table with a target lag time (e.g., TARGET_LAG = '1 MINUTE'). Snowflake will automatically compute the incremental changes on a continuous basis, vastly simplifying your code base. [6] 
* 

(Note: For row-by-row, ultra-low latency requirements from streaming platforms like Apache Kafka without writing to intermediate files, evaluate [Snowpipe Streaming](https://docs.snowflake.com/en/user-guide/data-load-snowpipe-streaming-overview), which bypasses file creation entirely to load data streams directly into table rows.) [3, 10] 
